    {
      "id": "IUINkvsQWFsT1PzZd8BOT",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 429.24237105273437,
      "y": 1701,
      "width": 626.0154418945312,
      "height": 80,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#868e96",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1Y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1801343594,
      "version": 748,
      "versionNonce": 967325802,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1795093501991,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Block (1, 0) fills the next portion the same way, with its own pieces: A1, A2\nand B3, B4. Blocks do not take turns: each has its own slice of shared memory.\nBlock (1,0) doesn't wait for Block (0,) to finish. All blocks in the wave\nrun at the same time, each working through its own phases.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Block (1, 0) fills the next portion the same way, with its own pieces: A1, A2\nand B3, B4. Blocks do not take turns: each has its own slice of shared memory.\nBlock (1,0) doesn't wait for Block (0,) to finish. All blocks in the wave\nrun at the same time, each working through its own phases.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Z1RA8VQf79sA_qVgTwxV6",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 1119.2884981269845,
      "y": 911.138888920635,
      "width": 778.4722222222217,
      "height": 789.8611111111107,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1Z",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 125505590,
      "version": 361,
      "versionNonce": 1215559402,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1795094780155,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Z0MGLELP2WEMiUS9LZvmK",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1472.8746147860554,
      "y": 908.6388889206349,
      "width": 71.29998779296875,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1a",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1590119210,
      "version": 178,
      "versionNonce": 896108970,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1795094780155,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "NOTES",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "NOTES",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ODtJ6IYX6u4aSnHJaVMIh",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1147.344799112227,
      "y": 937.7638884761905,
      "width": 722.359619140625,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1b",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 77994474,
      "version": 1620,
      "versionNonce": 2074002538,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1795094780155,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A and B stay whole in global memory.\nShared memory only ever holds a copy of one piece of each.\n\nA piece must be small enough to fit in shared memory.\nHere: 2x2 pieces of a 4x4 matrix (made-up small example).\n\nOne phase = load a pair, barrier, every thread computes from it.\nThe next phase overwrites the pair with the next one.\n\nC never goes into shared memory. Each thread keeps its running total\nin a register and writes its spot of C once, at the end.\n\nEvery block runs the same phases for its own portion of C, with\nits own pieces of A and B and its own slice of memory.\n\nRule of thumb: fetch from global once per piece, then read it\nfrom shared memory as many times as you need.\n\nTiling is a strategy to partition the data into subsets called tiles\nso that each file fits into the shared memory. \n\nThe term tile draws on the analogy that a large wall (global memory data)\ncan be covered by small tiles (subsets that each fit into the shared\nmemory). \n\nAn important criterion is that the kernel computation on these\ntiles can be independently of each other.\n\nNot all data structures can be partitioned can be partitioned\ninto tiles given an arbitrary kernel function.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A and B stay whole in global memory.\nShared memory only ever holds a copy of one piece of each.\n\nA piece must be small enough to fit in shared memory.\nHere: 2x2 pieces of a 4x4 matrix (made-up small example).\n\nOne phase = load a pair, barrier, every thread computes from it.\nThe next phase overwrites the pair with the next one.\n\nC never goes into shared memory. Each thread keeps its running total\nin a register and writes its spot of C once, at the end.\n\nEvery block runs the same phases for its own portion of C, with\nits own pieces of A and B and its own slice of memory.\n\nRule of thumb: fetch from global once per piece, then read it\nfrom shared memory as many times as you need.\n\nTiling is a strategy to partition the data into subsets called tiles\nso that each file fits into the shared memory. \n\nThe term tile draws on the analogy that a large wall (global memory data)\ncan be covered by small tiles (subsets that each fit into the shared\nmemory). \n\nAn important criterion is that the kernel computation on these\ntiles can be independently of each other.\n\nNot all data structures can be partitioned can be partitioned\ninto tiles given an arbitrary kernel function.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
